# Lines tools/qemu-test must see, in this order, as whole serial lines.
# Negative tests at the client's side of the virtio-net driver. Each
# probe holds the memory grants of the driver's client in net-test.system and
# makes one access they do not allow; each must fault, be caught by the
# parent and reported, and driver_side must answer after each one with its
# private page intact.
#
# The order follows from the priorities in net-isolation.system (one core).
# Fault lines name what was accessed and why it failed, not code addresses or
# translation levels, so they do not change from build to build; the "fault
# detail" lines that follow them carry those and are not checked.
NOWAYOS: isolation-parent: starting; children net_writes_driver_dma (0) and net_writes_rx_buffers (1)
NOWAYOS: isolation-bystander: starting; private page at 0x3000000 holds 0x5afec0de
NOWAYOS: isolation-probe: net_writes_driver_dma writing 0x3000000 (where the virtio-net driver maps its receive queue's DMA memory and driver_side keeps its private page), which is not mapped in this PD
NOWAYOS: isolation-parent: net_writes_driver_dma stopped: data write at 0x3000000: translation fault (nothing mapped there)
NOWAYOS: isolation-bystander: still running (check 1); private page intact
NOWAYOS: isolation-parent: bystander answered after fault 1
NOWAYOS: isolation-probe: net_writes_rx_buffers read 0x00000000 from its read-only receive buffers at 0x50000000
NOWAYOS: isolation-probe: net_writes_rx_buffers writing to its read-only receive buffers at 0x50000000
NOWAYOS: isolation-parent: net_writes_rx_buffers stopped: data write at 0x50000000: permission fault (the mapping does not allow it)
NOWAYOS: isolation-bystander: still running (check 2); private page intact
NOWAYOS: isolation-parent: bystander answered after fault 2
NOWAYOS: net-isolation: 2 of 2 forbidden accesses faulted and were contained; bystander still running

# Output that fails the test at once if it appears anywhere (substring match):
# a forbidden access that succeeded, a fault the monitor had to handle (a PD
# other than a probe faulted), a kernel error message (such as a fault reply
# left pending), a panic, or anything unexpected.
!ISOLATION FAILURE
!MON|ERROR
!seL4(CPU
!panicked
!CHANGED
!cannot
!unexpected
!NOT stopped
!no probe is defined
!no test is defined
